    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d522">

                <pb facs="0674" n="562" sameAs="frus:frus1918/pg562"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.0144/90</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Mexican Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bonillas</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Translation]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. A–1827</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-04-26">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 26, 1918</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: I have had the honor to receive
                    your excellency’s kind note dated April 17,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d522-f1"/> in
                    which you are pleased to inform me that the attention of the proper Department
                    of your Government has been called to the firing on Mexican soldiers who were at
                    Guadalupe, State of Chihuahua, by American soldiers during which a soldier, four
                    women, and a boy were killed and Maj. Porfirio García was wounded, to the end
                    that instructions to avoid a conflict be sent at the earliest possible date.</p>
                <p>To complement the investigations of the case and to prove that the American
                    forces started the firing without any cause, I enclose to your excellency a copy
                    of the reports made by the citizen Consul General at El Paso,<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d522-f2"/> who personally investigated the case and applied to Col.
                    C. T. Langhorne, commander of the military district of Big Bend, whom he asked
                    to punish the guilty.</p>
                <p>I avail [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Y. Bonillas</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
